00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.780
(FROM EMPIRE TO NEUTRALITY)
 &nbsp;

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:39.540
In culture and in the popular imagination we&nbsp;
tend to see Sweden as a calm and moderate state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:44.220
a bastion of social peace and a place&nbsp;
synonymous with modern social democracy.
 &nbsp;

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:49.200
Of course, this was not always the case. This&nbsp;
country’s past was much more... Intimidating.
 &nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:54.420
The Swedish Empire was one of the most powerful&nbsp;
European powers of the 17th century. At that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:59.280
time its territory also included present-day&nbsp;
Finland, Estonia and a large part of Norway.&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.240
But the question is, why are we telling you&nbsp;
this on VisualPolitik? Well, because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:07.800
configuration of the current territory in this&nbsp;
region of Europe responds to the events caused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:11.880
by what is known as the "Great Northern War".
 
We are talking about a conflict that took place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:18.120
between 1700 and 1721. During those years the&nbsp;
Swedish Empire maintained an armed conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.980
against Russia, Denmark and Norway. They were&nbsp;
fighting for supremacy in the Baltic Sea.
 &nbsp;

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:29.220
The conflict ended with the Treaty&nbsp;
of Nystad in 1721. With this treaty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:34.020
Sweden was beaten down by Russian tsarism.&nbsp;
And that's not all, a few decades later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:40.200
between 1808 and 1809 the so-called "Finnish&nbsp;
War" broke out where Russia again prevailed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:45.600
over Sweden. Following that war, the Grand Duchy&nbsp;
of Finland came under the control of Russia.
 &nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:52.020
And so, little by little, Russia became&nbsp;
a great Baltic power. This sea ceased to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:55.920
be a kind of exclusive lake of the&nbsp;
Swedish Empire and over time became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:59.160
the natural outlet of the Russian&nbsp;
fleet to the seas of the world.
 &nbsp;

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:07.440
For its part, Sweden, starting in the&nbsp;
19th century and after having lost its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:11.940
former imperial grandeur, adopted a policy of&nbsp;
neutrality. The reorganization of the State&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:15.540
and the development of the country became&nbsp;
its main priorities. We are talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:20.760
a neutrality that for more than 150 years was&nbsp;
basically only called into question during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:25.980
Second World War. Questioned but not broken.&nbsp;
Officially, Sweden did not join either side.
 &nbsp;

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:31.320
And later, in 1949, when World War Two had&nbsp;
already given way to the Cold War, Stockholm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:36.840
opted to stay out of NATO and declared a security&nbsp;
policy based on independence and neutrality.
 &nbsp;

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:45.600
In the 1990s, however, a wave of pro-European&nbsp;
enthusiasm swept over Sweden, so that in 1995&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:50.880
the country finally decided to break with its&nbsp;
historical State policy and join the European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.260
Union. What’s more, although it remained&nbsp;
outside NATO, it did strengthen its ties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.260 --> 00:05:00.840
with this organization. For example, during all&nbsp;
these years it has participated as an "affiliate",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:07.200
as an "ally" but not as a member in NATO missions&nbsp;
in Bosnia, Kosovo, Afghanistan and Libya.
 &nbsp;

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:14.820
And there’s even more. In 2007, Sweden&nbsp;
joined Finland, Norway, Estonia, Ireland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:21.240
Latvia and Lithuania in the Nordic Battle Group&nbsp;
(NBG), one of the European Union's battle groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:26.460
whose headquarters are in the Swedish town of&nbsp;
Enköping. A reserve group of 2,400 soldiers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:34.140
from these countries is based there.
 
In other words, Swedish neutrality began to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:39.600
disappear at full speed. Something that, believe&nbsp;
it or not, Vladimir Putin had nothing to do with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:44.580
And what, you may ask, did the Russian leader&nbsp;
do? Well, basically, he did what he does best:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:49.980
he launched a constant campaign of intimidation.&nbsp;
His goal? To prevent Sweden from joining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:53.520
the Atlantic Alliance at all costs.
